    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle, we start by recalling that the sum of the interior angles of any triangle is always 180 degrees. Let's denote the measures of the three interior angles of a triangle as \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[A + B + C = 180^\\circ\\]\n\nThe mean value of these angles is calculated by dividing the sum of the angles by the number of angles, which is 3. So, the mean value is:\n\n\\[\\text{Mean} = \\frac{A + B + C}{3}\\]\n\nSubstituting the sum of the angles, we get:\n\n\\[\\text{Mean} = \\frac{180^\\circ}{3} = 60^\\circ\\]\n\nThus, the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle is \\(\\boxed{60}\\).",
