      "text": "To find the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle, we can use the fact that the sum of the interior angles of any triangle is always \\(180^\\circ\\).\n\nLet's denote the measures of the three interior angles of a triangle as \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\). According to the properties of triangles, we have:\n\\[ A + B + C = 180^\\circ \\]\n\nThe mean value of the measures of the three interior angles is given by the formula for the arithmetic mean:\n\\[ \\text{Mean} = \\frac{A + B + C}{3} \\]\n\nSubstituting the sum of the angles into the formula, we get:\n\\[ \\text{Mean} = \\frac{180^\\circ}{3} \\]\n\nNow, we perform the division:\n\\[ \\text{Mean} = 60^\\circ \\]\n\nTherefore, the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle is \\(\\boxed{60}\\).",
